# Review: one procedure for every agent

Status: stable reference (R-0544)

Every branch is reviewed by `scripts/review/review.sh` before it lands. The
script picks the diff, splits it, runs the reviewer, records the verdict as a
git note on the reviewed commit, and enforces the round cap.
`scripts/review/verify.sh` validates that note. The landing tools in `scripts/land/`
([LANDING.md](LANDING.md)) read its verdict through `scripts/land/notes.sh`: a branch is
ready to land only when its head carries a clean review note, and the landing bot carries
a clean verdict across a conflict-free rebase.

## Why one script

Measured on 2026-10-22:

- Reviews took 8 to 34 rounds per branch, and every round re-reviewed the whole
  growing diff. Most of each round's cost was re-reading code that an earlier
  round had already accepted.
- The reviewer's content filter refused whole rounds on ownership and memory
  diffs. The same diffs went through once the prompt described the work neutrally
  ("a compiler ownership-checker review") and the diff was sent in smaller pieces.

Each agent had been solving both problems by hand, differently. The script does
it the same way every time, and its note is the record the landing bot checks.

## Usage

```sh
scripts/review/review.sh [--base <ref>] [--head <ref>] [--max-rounds 5] [--pieces auto|N] [--prompt-extra "..."]
scripts/review/verify.sh [--fetch] <commit>
```

One invocation runs **one round** on `HEAD`. Commit fixes between rounds; the
uncommitted tree is never reviewed, and the script warns when it is dirty.

| option | meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--base <ref>` | base of round 1. Default: the merge-base of `HEAD` with `origin/main`. |
| `--head <ref>` | the commit the caller means to review; it must be the checkout's HEAD, or nothing runs (exit 2). `train.sh` and `ready.sh` pass it. |
| `--max-rounds N` | the round cap (default 5). |
| `--pieces auto\|N` | `auto` splits at 60000 bytes per piece (`REVIEW_PIECE_BYTES`); `N` aims for N pieces. File groups are never merged, so a diff touching more groups than N still gets one piece per group. |
| `--prompt-extra "..."` | text appended to every prompt, such as what the branch is for. |

| exit | meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | clean |
| 1 | findings: fix, commit, run again |
| 2 | usage or environment error; nothing recorded |
| 3 | round cap reached; split the branch or escalate (open findings are printed) |
| 4 | nothing to review: `HEAD` was already reviewed with findings, or the diff is empty |
| 5 | blocked: a piece could not be reviewed, even after the retry |
| 6 | recorded locally, but the notes ref could not be pushed |

## What each round reviews

- **Round 1** reviews the full diff from the base to `HEAD`.
- **Round N > 1** reviews only the delta since the last reviewed commit. The
  script finds that commit by walking the branch (`base..HEAD`) for the newest
  note under `refs/notes/concrete-review`. The prompt includes the previous
  round's findings and asks the reviewer to list each one that is still open.
- A `blocked` round does not count as having reviewed its diff, so the next
  round starts from the last `clean` or `findings` round, or from the base.
  Running again on a blocked `HEAD` is allowed and counts as a round. A round
  with both findings and a blocked piece is `blocked` for this reason; its
  findings are still recorded and carried into the next prompt, together with
  those of the last fully reviewed round.
- A `HEAD` that is already `clean` exits 0 without calling the reviewer.

Rounds are counted from the notes, so the cap holds across invocations,
worktrees and agents. Rebasing creates new commits without notes, which
restarts the count; say so when you hand the branch over.

## Splitting and the neutral prompt

The diff is split by file group first, in this order: compiler sources
(`Concrete/`, `std/`, `proofs/`, `grammar/`, top-level Lean and Lake files),
tests (`tests/`, `lean_tests/`, `examples/`, `bench/`, `scripts/tests/`),
scripts (everything else), docs (`docs/`, `paper/`, `site/`, `research/`,
`*.md`). Within a group, files are packed into pieces at or under the size
limit. A file larger than the limit is cut at hunk boundaries, and a hunk larger
than the limit is cut into parts with renumbered `@@` headers, so each piece is a
well-formed diff. `scripts/review/split_diff.py` does the splitting.

The prompt describes the repository as a compiler and asks for correctness
findings: wrong behaviour, logic errors, regressions, unhandled cases, tests that
do not check what they claim, and docs that contradict the code. It runs
`codex exec --sandbox read-only` from the repository root, with the piece on
stdin, so the reviewer can open other files for context but cannot change them.
It runs with `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`: the sandbox does not stop git from taking the
worktree's `index.lock` for an index refresh, and a reviewer command stopped
mid-refresh left that lock behind (bug 209).

The reply must end with a line that is exactly `VERDICT: clean` or
`VERDICT: findings`. Markdown emphasis around it is tolerated; anything after it
is not. A reply without that line is treated as no answer, never as clean.

**Refusals.** A piece that comes back refused, with no verdict line, or with a
reviewer error is retried **once**: the piece is halved and each half is sent
with a more neutral prompt that leaves out the project framing. If any half
fails again, the piece is `blocked` and the script moves on. It does not loop.
Refusal text is matched only when the reply has no verdict line, so a real
review that discusses content filters still parses.

## Owner decisions: choosing versus recording

Both prompts ask whether the patch picks a side on an owner decision: choosing, revising,
encoding, implementing, extending or reverting what an undecided question covers, whether one of
the language decisions OD1 – OD157 ([decisions.md](decisions.md#owner-decisions)) or an important
project question. That is a finding, and the patch does not land: only the owner decides.

Recording a decision the owner explicitly approved, under the
[recording rule](decisions.md#decided-project-recording), is not picking a side, and the review
permits it. For such a patch the reviewer checks four things, and a failure of any is a finding:

- **provenance**: each recorded answer carries the owner's answer, quoted with its date, or the
  quoted owner rule that decides the question under the recording rule;
- **faithful transcription**: the recorded text says what that answer says, no more and no less;
- **scope**: the patch changes only what that answer covers;
- **nothing unapproved**: nothing else is marked decided, and no undecided question is chosen or
  revised.

A review that refuses such a patch is answered in the next round (a quoted answer, a narrower
change), never by skipping the review.

## The note

After each round the script writes this note on `HEAD` under
`refs/notes/concrete-review`, and pushes the ref to `origin`:

```
verdict: clean|findings|blocked
rounds: N
reviewer: codex
utc: 2026-10-22T14:03:11Z
base: <40-hex sha>
pieces: K
```

**The format is an interface.** The landing bot parses it, and `verify.sh`
rejects any note that lacks one of these six keys, repeats a key, or carries a
key other than these and the landing pipeline's two optional ones
(`carried-from: <sha>`, which the bot writes when it carries a clean verdict
across a conflict-free rebase, and `order: N`). Keys are matched by name, not
position. Change the format only together with both.

- `rounds` is this round's number, which is also the number of rounds run on the
  branch so far.
- `base` is the branch base (the merge-base), not the start of the delta.
- `pieces` counts the pieces before any refusal retry.

When the verdict is not clean, the findings text goes in a second note under
`refs/notes/concrete-review-findings`, which is pushed too. It is how the next
round, possibly run by another agent, gets the previous findings. The full raw
output of every round is written to `.review/<sha>-<round>.md`, which git
ignores.

A non-fast-forward rejection (another agent pushed notes in the meantime) is
handled by fetching the remote notes, merging them (`git notes merge -s ours`: on
a conflict for the same commit the local, newer note wins), and retrying, up to
three attempts. Any other push failure stops at once with exit 6. The script also
fetches and merges the remote notes before each round, so it counts rounds that
were run elsewhere.

The pre-push hook (`.githooks/pre-push`) runs no gates for a push that updates
only these two notes refs: a notes commit holds one blob per annotated commit and
no source tree. A push that also carries a branch takes that branch's tier.
Until this hook reaches `main`, a hook installed from an older `main` still runs
the gates on a note push, and the push fails outside `nix develop`.

## The cap

At most `--max-rounds` rounds (default 5). When the last allowed round ends with
findings or blocked pieces, the script records the note, prints the open
findings, and exits 3. Asking for another round exits 3 without calling the
reviewer. Do not work around the cap: split the branch into smaller branches or
escalate to a human reviewer, and pass on the open findings.

## verify.sh

```sh
scripts/review/verify.sh <commit>          # exit 0 clean, 1 findings/blocked, 2 none or malformed
scripts/review/verify.sh --fetch <commit>  # fetch the notes ref from origin first
```

It judges the exact commit. A clean note on an ancestor says nothing about the
commits after it, so a branch that gains a commit after its clean round needs
another round.

## Tests

`scripts/checks/review_tool.sh` runs the scripts against throwaway
repositories, with a bare repository as `origin` and a fake `codex` that answers
according to markers in the diff. It needs no API key. It covers delta selection,
splitting, the refusal retry, the cap, note writing and pushing (including the
non-fast-forward merge), verdict parsing, and `verify.sh`.
## Gates (CI jobs, added to `required-checks.txt`)

1. **Compiler build:** `lake build Concrete`, with warnings as errors and `autoImplicit` off.
2. **Axioms and trust:** extended to `Concrete/`:
   - only the three axioms;
   - no `sorry` and no `native_decide`/`ofReduceBool`;
   - a **totality ban**: no `partial`, `unsafe`, `@[implemented_by]`, `@[extern]` or `opaque` in `Concrete/` outside `Premises.lean`;
   - an external-checker replay (con-leche/nanoda, R-0553) of the compiler's theorems.

   Not yet a job. Until it is, the trusted-base golden (gate 9) fixes each headline theorem's axioms (a `sorryAx` among them fails it), and the trust ban of `scripts/proofs/trust_ban.sh` covers the specification only: it reads the compiler's library entry and its pinned dependency in `lakefile.toml`, not its sources or constants.
3. **Premise registry and stage coverage:** the checks described in the stage registry section above.
4. **Conformance, both backends:** each `tests/slice0/*.con` example runs through:
   - the Lean `run`;
   - the LLVM-native executable;
   - the LCB-native executable.

   All three must give the same observation, and each must match the `.expect`.

   **Classification uses the status and the runtime evidence, never the status alone.** `Observation.status` is many-to-one.
   - Status 134 **with** the runtime's trap line on fd 2 is `trapped k`.
   - Status 134 **without** it is `exited 134`.
   - Status 125 **with** the launcher's refusal line is `startupRefused`.
   - Status 125 **without** it is `exited 125`.

   Accepted examples `return 125;` and `return 134;` are in the corpus and must classify as exits. Slice 0 can't produce a real startup refusal, because it has no grants. The refusal and trap classifications are also exercised by comparator self-tests: stub images, and the trap via gate 6.

   **Refused examples have their own leg.** The Lean `check` result, the compiler's refusal (with its rule) and the refusal certificate must agree with `.expect`. No executable exists for a refused example. A separate supported-subset coverage report lists every grammar production and Core form that the corpus exercises. A reject-all compiler fails, because every accepted example must exit with its status.
5. **Differential at the source:** generated accepted programs, with replayable seeds; native exit = Lean `run`.
6. **Differential through the token-free lowering, both backends.** This covers overflow. Generated **well-scoped, unchecked** slice-0 programs over **arbitrary** `u8` operands, including `200 + 56`, take `lower q ∅ → memory → ssa`, so they have no tokens and no checker. Their Core `run` is compared with the LLVM path and the LCB path. Overflow must give exit 134 and the right site's trap line on fd 2, for both backends. This exercises token-aware lowering, Memory CFG and SSA, not only the backends.
   A second leg compares generated BIR directly with both backends, to cover BIR shapes that lowering does not produce.

   An in-process leg also compares `BIR.behaviour b` with LCB's own evaluator on the emitted body, an executable cross-check of `emit_correct`.
6a. **AArch64:** a GitHub-hosted AArch64 macOS job (`macos-15`) builds the LCB output for `aarch64-macos`, runs gates 4 and 6 there natively, and compares against Lean. LLVM-native also runs there, as a third cross-check. Gates 4 and 6 for x86-64 run on concrete-ci-2.
7. **Certificates:** the kernel checks every acceptance and refusal certificate, with measured budgets.
8. **Reproducibility** (implemented, `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_repro.sh`): two independent builds give byte-identical outputs at every stage, on each target, and two independent Linux runners give identical manifests. See [Reproducible builds](#reproducible-builds-owner-2026-11-07).
9. **Trusted base** (owner, 2026-11-07): a golden test that `concrete facts` prints exactly the spec's premise list, so a premise added, removed or reworded anywhere fails until the golden is updated with it. Implemented provisionally in `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_trusted_base.sh`: until `concrete facts` exists, `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_trusted_base.lean` prints `Premises.report`: the premise registry `Concrete/Premises.lean`, the sorted axioms of each headline theorem (`Premises.theorems`) and each axiom's type; the gate appends the Lean toolchain and the Lake pins, and the result must equal `tests/golden/slice0_trusted_base.txt` byte for byte. The README's open-premise table must equal the registry's rendering (`Premises.tableRow`). Controls cover the comparison, a failing printer, the axiom collection and a drifted table. The statements are text until `FrontendPremises` and `NativePremises` land as Lean structures; each entry then names its field.
10. **Sanitizers** (owner, 2026-11-07; UBSan dropped by the owner, 2026-11-08; implemented in `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_artifacts.sh`): every LLVM example also runs built with ASan in CI, and must give the same observation with no sanitizer report. ASan instruments only functions marked `sanitize_address`, which clang's C front end adds and the printed IR lacks, so the sanitizer build adds it to every definition (`slice0_native.sh sanitize`); a control body with an out-of-bounds load must be reported. There is no UBSan leg: UBSan's checks are inserted by clang's C front end, so on LLVM IR input it checks nothing. No C is compiled in slice 0, and the LLVM-level undefined behaviour of the printed IR (poison, `unreachable`) stays P_print's.
11. **Binary size** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented, same script): the size of each example's executable (bytes and text size, pinned `llvm-size`), per backend and target, is recorded per run in `sizes.tsv`, uploaded with the job. Report-only: it never fails a run. **Compile time** (owner, 2026-11-08; same script, report-only): each example's emit step (the Lean emitter, Lean start-up included) and native step (clang and LCB's command line) in wall-clock milliseconds, next to its budget (the step's time bound), in `compile_times.tsv`.
12. **Stack bound** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented in the same script and `lib/stack_bound.py`; the `aarch64-macos` run check with LLDB, 2026-11-08): a stack-bound fact for each example, derived from the final artifact (the linked executable, not the IR) for both backends, and checked against a run. The analysis follows every path of the Concrete-owned code, instruction by instruction from an explicit set of modelled instructions, (`main`, `concrete_rt_write_all`, `concrete_body`) over the disassembly's control-flow graph, tracking the stack and frame pointers' depth below `main`'s entry and every byte addressed relative to them (so the red zone), refusing an access deeper than the target's red zone below the stack pointer (128 bytes on x86-64 System V and on Apple arm64, none under AAPCS64 on Linux: deeper bytes may be overwritten by a signal handler), and fails closed on anything it does not model (an instruction outside the set, another stack-pointer write, frame- or link-register writeback, an indirect branch or call, a stack pointer copied out, an indexed stack address, a stack read of bytes the function has not itself written on every path since its last call (so no stale saved frame pointer is ever loaded), a saved slot read other than by the modelled restore, two depths meeting, recursion, a call to a function neither owned nor a PLT/stub). The external calls (`write`, `_exit`, `abort`) are listed with the depth at their entry; their own use, and that `_exit` and `abort` do not return, are the libc premise. On x86-64 Linux the run check single-steps the owned code under the pinned gdb and requires the measured depth to be within the bound, for a complete run only (stopped first at `main`, back at each external call's return address, no signal, the expected exit status). The measured depth is the lowest of the stack pointer and every stack byte a stepped instruction addresses (`lib/stack_access.py` evaluates each memory operand), so a red-zone access is counted, and one deeper than the red zone is an error. A control links a `main` that stores at the red zone's edge without moving the stack pointer and requires bound 128 and measured 128 (on AArch64 it first allocates and frees a 144-byte frame with a pre-indexed store, judged against the new stack pointer, so 144); with a 120-byte red zone the same run gives no measurement. On Mach-O each stub entry's imported symbol comes from the indirect symbol table (`llvm-objdump --macho --indirect-symbols`), since the disassembly names only the `__stubs` section. On `aarch64-macos` the run check is the same, under Apple's LLDB (`/usr/bin/lldb`, `lib/stack_measure_lldb.py`; an external call's return address is the link register); LLDB is not pinned (Apple's signed debugserver is the only one allowed to attach), so its version is printed in the gate log. `stack.tsv` records bound, measurement and externals.

With the front end (owner, 2026-11-07): gate 5's random-program fuzzer compares the native executables with the Lean evaluator, and a JSON schema generated from the Lean types of the `facts` output has goldens. Each gate joins `required-checks.txt` when the compiler lands.

